\title{Description Complexity, Logical Erasure, and the\\Thermodynamic Cost of Proof}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a mathematical framework for relating proof descriptions to thermodynamic information cost. For a proof object $\pi$, a fixed prefix-free universal description language, and temperature parameter $T\geq 0$, we define the description-erasure scale $C_T(\pi)=T\ln(2)K(\pi)$, where $K(\pi)$ is prefix Kolmogorov complexity. Lower description complexity immediately implies no greater cost. A prefix-free counting argument shows that short descriptions are scarce in finite ensembles. For a uniformly sampled family of $2^n$ distinct objects, this yields mean complexity $\Theta(n)$ under a natural $O(n)$ encoding assumption, and hence mean cost $\Theta(Tn)$. Thus a cost functional linear in description length does not have an undifferentiated $\Theta(2^n)$ average under the uniform binary model; exponential behavior instead arises naturally in exhaustive proof search. Comparison sorting provides an exact model: $n!$ controls decision-tree depth, information erased by sorting, and reversible history capacity, while redundant comparisons show that operation count is not itself Landauer cost. We conclude with a precise conjectural program for shortest-proof complexity and memory--dissipation tradeoffs.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Information processing is physical. Bits occupy distinguishable physical states, and resetting a memory can merge states that were previously distinct. Landauer's principle associates the erasure of one unbiased bit at absolute temperature $T$ with the ideal energy scale $k_{\mathrm B}T\ln 2$. When temperature is measured in energy units, this is written $T\ln 2$.

A proof is an information-bearing object, so one may associate a Landauer scale with a shortest description of that proof. The natural formula is
\[
C_T(\pi)=T\ln(2)K(\pi),
\]
where $K(\pi)$ is the length of a shortest self-delimiting program producing $\pi$. The formula is coherent, but its interpretation must be limited carefully. It does not by itself state the energy used to discover $\pi$, the energy used by a particular verifier, or an unavoidable heat cost for every physical realization. It is a benchmark for erasing a shortest description.

Four quantities must be distinguished:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \emph{description complexity}, the bit length of a shortest program generating a proof;
\item \emph{search complexity}, the resources consumed while finding a proof;
\item \emph{logical erasure}, the distinctions merged by an irreversible map; and
\item \emph{physical dissipation}, the heat produced by a concrete device.
\end{enumerate}
The first is an object measure, the second a process measure, the third a logical and distributional measure, and the fourth depends on hardware and protocol. Their separation is the organizing principle of this paper.

Our conclusions are both positive and corrective. The proposed cost is monotone in $K$. Finite counting forces incompressible members in large ensembles. Under uniform binary sampling, however, average description complexity grows linearly with statement length, not exponentially. Exponential growth naturally belongs to proof search. A sorting model makes the same distinctions exact and elementary.

\section{Definitions and conventions}

\subsection{Prefix descriptions}

Fix a universal prefix-free binary machine $U$. Prefix-free means that no valid program is a proper prefix of another valid program.

\begin{definition}[Prefix Kolmogorov complexity]
For a finite binary object $x$, define
\[
K_U(x)=\min\{\lvert p\rvert:U(p)=x\}.
\]
When $U$ is fixed, write $K(x)$.
\end{definition}

Changing the universal machine changes complexity by at most an additive constant independent of $x$. Exact values are language-dependent, while asymptotic statements stable under additive constants are robust.

\begin{definition}[Proof system]
A proof system is a decidable relation $V(s,\pi)$ between encoded statements $s$ and finite proof objects $\pi$. If $V(s,\pi)$ holds, then $\pi$ proves $s$. The system is sound if every accepted statement is true in its intended semantics.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Shortest-proof description complexity]
For a provable statement $s$, define
\[
K_{\mathrm{prf}}(s)=\min\{K(\pi):V(s,\pi)\}.
\]
\end{definition}
This differs from shortest written length. A repetitive derivation may have many symbols but a small generating program.

\subsection{The description-erasure scale}

\begin{definition}[Description-erasure scale]
For $T\geq 0$ and a proof $\pi$, define
\[
C_T(\pi)=T\ln(2)K(\pi).
\]
For a provable statement $s$, define
\[
C_T^{\min}(s)=T\ln(2)K_{\mathrm{prf}}(s).
\]
\end{definition}

If temperature $T_{\mathrm K}$ is in kelvin, $T$ is replaced by $k_{\mathrm B}T_{\mathrm K}$. The term ``scale'' emphasizes that Landauer's principle concerns actual logical erasure under physical assumptions. Reversible devices may retain information and postpone erasure; practical devices may dissipate far more than the ideal minimum.

\subsection{Finite ensembles and search}

For a finite nonempty set $S$ under the uniform distribution, define
\[
\overline K(S)=\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{x\in S}K(x),
\qquad
\overline C_T(S)=T\ln(2)\overline K(S).
\]
A description search through length $n$ examines candidate programs of length at most $n$. There are $2^{n+1}-1$ binary strings in that range, so exhaustive search can be exponential even when the successful description has linear length.

\section{Monotonicity and language dependence}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity]
Let $T\geq 0$. If $K(\pi_1)\leq K(\pi_2)$, then
\[
C_T(\pi_1)\leq C_T(\pi_2).
\]
If $T>0$, equality holds precisely when $K(\pi_1)=K(\pi_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The multiplier $T\ln 2$ is nonnegative, so multiplication preserves order. If $T>0$, the multiplier is strictly positive and reflects equality.
\end{proof}

Thus the slogan ``shorter proofs cost less'' is valid when shorter means lower minimal description complexity. It is not established merely by comparing the visible symbol counts of two derivations.

\begin{proposition}[Change of universal language]
For any two universal prefix-free machines $U$ and $U'$, there is a constant $a\geq 0$ such that, for every proof $\pi$,
\[
|C_{T,U}(\pi)-C_{T,U'}(\pi)|\leq T\ln(2)a.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The invariance theorem gives constants bounding each complexity by the other plus an additive overhead. Take the larger overhead and multiply by $T\ln 2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Finite incompressibility}

\begin{lemma}[Prefix counting]
For every integer $m\geq 0$, fewer than $2^m$ objects satisfy $K(x)<m$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Every such object has a program shorter than $m$. For prefix-free programs, Kraft's inequality states
\[
\sum_p2^{-|p|}\leq 1.
\]
Each program shorter than $m$ contributes more than $2^{-m}$, so fewer than $2^m$ such programs exist. Choosing one shortest program for each object gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite ensemble incompressibility]
Let $S$ contain $N$ distinct objects. For every $m\geq 0$,
\[
|\{x\in S:K(x)\geq m\}|>N-2^m.
\]
If $N=2^n$ and $1\leq c\leq n$, more than a fraction $1-2^{-c}$ of $S$ satisfies
\[
K(x)\geq n-c.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the prefix counting lemma, fewer than $2^m$ members have complexity below $m$. Subtract from $N$. For the second statement set $m=n-c$ and divide by $2^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear mean cost]
Suppose $S_n$ consists of $2^n$ distinct objects and a fixed decoder reconstructs every member from an $n$-bit index with at most $d$ additional self-delimiting bits, where $d$ is independent of $n$. Then
\[
\overline K(S_n)=\Theta(n).
\]
For fixed $T>0$,
\[
\overline C_T(S_n)=\Theta(Tn).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For any fixed $c\geq 1$, incompressibility shows that more than a fraction $1-2^{-c}$ has complexity at least $n-c$. Hence
\[
\overline K(S_n)>(1-2^{-c})(n-c),
\]
a linear lower bound. The decoder gives $K(x)\leq n+d$ for every member and hence a linear upper bound. Multiplication by $T\ln 2$ gives the cost statement.
\end{proof}

This result corrects an unconditional exponential-average claim. The existence of $2^n$ objects forces typical descriptions to have roughly $n$ bits, not $2^n$ bits. Different theorem distributions can behave differently, but their assumptions must be stated explicitly.

\section{Description length versus search}

\begin{proposition}[Exponential candidate space]
The number of binary strings of length at most $n$ is
\[
\sum_{j=0}^n2^j=2^{n+1}-1.
\]
Consequently, naive exhaustive testing through length $n$ has a candidate space of size $\Theta(2^n)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
There are $2^j$ strings of length $j$; sum the geometric series.
\end{proof}

Prefix-free validity may discard candidates, but it does not generally transform the worst-case exponential search into a linear process. Thus a typical $n$-bit object can have $\Theta(n)$ description length while the process of locating its description inspects $\Theta(2^n)$ candidates. If rejected candidates are irreversibly reset, dissipation can grow with the explored tree. Reversible search can reduce erasure by retaining history, at the expense of memory and time.

\section{Comparison sorting as an exact model}

Sorting distinct objects gives a finite model in which every multiplicity is explicit.

\begin{definition}[Binary comparison tree]
A comparison tree is either a leaf or a branch with two subtrees. Define leaves and height recursively by
\[
L(\mathrm{leaf})=1,
\quad
L(\mathrm{branch}(u,v))=L(u)+L(v),
\]
\[
h(\mathrm{leaf})=0,
\quad
h(\mathrm{branch}(u,v))=1+\max\{h(u),h(v)\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Leaf--height bound]
Every binary tree satisfies
\[
L(t)\leq 2^{h(t)}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induct on the tree. The leaf case is equality. At a branch, apply the inductive bounds to both children. Each child has height at most one below the parent, so their leaves total at most $2^{h(t)-1}+2^{h(t)-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Comparison lower bound]
Any binary comparison tree that distinguishes all $n!$ input permutations has
\[
h(t)\geq\lceil\log_2(n!)\rceil.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The tree needs at least $n!$ leaves. The leaf--height bound gives $n!\leq 2^{h(t)}$. Take base-two logarithms and the ceiling.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact sorting erasure scale]
Under the uniform distribution on input permutations, a sorting map that retains only the sorted output forgets exactly $\log_2(n!)$ bits. Its ideal Landauer scale is
\[
T\ln(2)\log_2(n!)=T\ln(n!).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
All $n!$ input permutations produce the same ordered arrangement. A uniform label among $n!$ possibilities has entropy $\log_2(n!)$. The energy identity follows from change of logarithm base.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reversible history lower bound]
Any reversible implementation whose visible output is the sorted sequence must possess at least $n!$ distinguishable auxiliary states over that output fiber.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A reversible map is injective. Since all $n!$ visible outputs agree, the auxiliary components corresponding to the $n!$ distinct inputs must be pairwise distinct.
\end{proof}

The same factorial therefore controls comparison transcripts, erased information, and reversible history capacity.

\begin{definition}[Redundant padding]
For a tree $t$, define $P_0(t)=t$ and
\[
P_{r+1}(t)=\mathrm{branch}(P_r(t),P_r(t)).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Padding separates comparisons from erasure]
For every $r\geq 0$,
\[
h(P_r(t))=r+h(t),
\]
and $P_r(t)$ has at least as many leaves as $t$. Thus padding preserves sufficient transcript capacity for sorting while increasing worst-case comparisons by exactly $r$. The sorting map, its erased permutation information, and its ideal Landauer scale remain unchanged.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $r$ gives the height identity. Each padding step doubles the leaf count. Padding modifies the decision process but not the intended input--output sorting function, so the logical information lost by that function is unchanged.
\end{proof}

This theorem disproves the unqualified equation ``one comparison equals one erased bit.'' A physical comparison may consume energy, but its cost requires a model of circuitry and reset operations.

\section{Numerical algorithms}

Three diagnostics illustrate the theory. First, for given $n$ and $T$, compute $\ln(n!)$ using $\operatorname{lgamma}(n+1)$, then obtain the erased information $\ln(n!)/\ln 2$, the comparison lower bound by a ceiling, and the Landauer scale $T\ln(n!)$. This uses constant working space in a fixed-precision special-function model.

Second, for ensemble exponent $n$ and deficiency $c$, compute the guaranteed incompressible fraction $1-2^{-c}$ and the mean lower bound $(1-2^{-c})(n-c)$. These are theorem-driven bounds, not attempts to calculate uncomputable exact complexities.

Third, compare the linear description scale $n$ with the exhaustive candidate count $2^{n+1}-1$. The integer output itself has only $\Theta(n)$ bits even though it represents an exponentially large candidate family.

\section{Applications to proof systems and machine learning}

In automated reasoning, a generator proposes proof objects and a verifier checks them. The shortest description of a successful proof, the number of proposals considered, and the information erased when buffers are reset can differ by exponential factors. Evaluation should therefore report proof size, search effort, verifier work, and retained history separately.

The same separation appears in machine learning. A compact trained model may require expensive optimization. Model description length is not training energy. Compression can reduce storage and communication, but locating the compressed representation and decompressing it have their own costs. Inference hardware may repeatedly overwrite registers even when the abstract function admits a reversible extension.

For a deterministic finite map $f:X\to Y$, the fiber $f^{-1}(y)$ measures multiplicity. Under a uniform distribution on that fiber, forgetting the input label loses $\log_2|f^{-1}(y)|$ bits. A reversible extension needs at least $|f^{-1}(y)|$ distinguishable histories. Verification maps often have large fibers: many malformed candidates yield the same rejection verdict, and many encodings may express the same argument. Physical consequences depend on when those distinctions are erased.

\section{A conjectural shortest-proof theorem}

Finite incompressibility does not alone prove that shortest proofs in a fixed arithmetic theory escape every computable bound. Such a theorem needs effective syntax, soundness, a specified proof relation, and a bridge from statement length to certified incompressibility.

\begin{conjecture}[Computable-bound escape]
Fix a sound, computably axiomatized theory capable of elementary arithmetic and a prefix-free universal description language. There is no computable function $g$ such that every theorem of statement length at most $n$ has a proof $\pi$ with
\[
K(\pi)\leq g(n).
\]
\end{conjecture}

A Chaitin-style strategy would assume such a bound, use it to search a finite proof space effectively, and diagonalize to produce an object certified to have complexity exceeding the bound used to locate it. The hard points are expressibility of complexity assertions, internal proof of search correctness, and the use of soundness to pass from internal certification to external truth.

Even if proved, the conjecture would not imply exponential mean cost for uniformly sampled statements. Nonexistence of a uniform computable bound, ensemble averages, and search-time lower bounds are distinct assertions.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

Exact Kolmogorov complexity is uncomputable. Numerical work can evaluate explicit code lengths and rigorous counting bounds, but not $K(x)$ for arbitrary $x$. Moreover, Landauer's factor is an ideal lower scale for erasure, not a complete hardware energy model. Error correction, finite-time operation, leakage, communication, and clocking can dominate practical energy use.

Probability models also matter. Uniform strings, true statements, curriculum-generated theorems, and model-generated proofs induce different distributions. Any average claim must state its ensemble. These limitations prevent a useful metaphor from turning into a false identity. Description complexity captures compressibility; search complexity captures discovery; reversible computation trades retained history for reduced erasure; and device physics determines actual heat.

\section{Future directions}

The first goal is to establish computable-bound escape for a concrete arithmetic proof system with explicit encodings and soundness assumptions. The second is to separate linear mean description cost from exponential mean proof-search work under natural theorem distributions. The third is to quantify a reversible verification space--heat frontier using the largest verifier fibers. The fourth is to combine propositional proof-size lower bounds with ancillary-memory constraints to derive thermodynamic lower bounds for irreversible verification.

An empirical program should compare proof compressors, search algorithms, and reversible verifier designs. Since exact Kolmogorov complexity is inaccessible, experiments should report explicit compression upper bounds and structural or counting lower bounds.

\section{Conclusion}

The formula $C_T(\pi)=T\ln(2)K(\pi)$ supports a rigorous but limited theorem: a proof with lower prefix complexity has a lower description-erasure scale. Prefix counting shows that most members of a uniform $2^n$-object ensemble require nearly $n$ bits, yielding linear mean cost under an $O(n)$ encoding. The exponential quantity $2^n$ naturally measures an exhaustive candidate space, not the average description length.

Sorting gives an exact analogue. The multiplicity $n!$ yields a comparison lower bound $\lceil\log_2(n!)\rceil$, erasure scale $T\ln(n!)$, and reversible history requirement of at least $n!$ states. Redundant padding increases comparison depth without changing logical erasure, proving that operation count and Landauer cost are not interchangeable.

A thermodynamic theory of proof should therefore track which distinctions a computation merges, which histories a reversible implementation retains, and which candidate spaces a discovery process explores. Proof length, search work, logical erasure, and physical heat are related only through explicit models.

